From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 2 Sep 2026 03:10:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] drm: h713-afbd: record what the flip timing measured

Two things in the video plane's commit path look expensive enough to cost
frames, and measurement says neither does. Write that down at the call sites,
because the next reader will otherwise re-derive the same suspicion and "fix"
working code -- which is exactly the reasoning this patch exists to stop.

atomic_update() rewrites all per-stream state on every commit (geometry, both
strides, the ring-mux word, all four queue slots, the dirty latch) when a
page-flip only needs the Y/C addresses. wait_ready() is called twice on enable.
Yet the plane sustains 59.71 fps on a 59.97 Hz panel with every flip on its own
vsync -- n=298, sd 0.03 ms, zero drops -- and PTS-paced 29.97 fps content runs
with nothing late.

No behavioural change. An attempt at one is recorded in the comment because it
failed usefully: the wait_ready() bound was tightened from 50 ms to 2 ms on the
theory that the latch clears in ~75 us. That figure is DECD's ring-arm timing,
carried across by analogy, and it is wrong for this register. On hardware it
produced 275 "READY did not clear" warnings in a single 10-second run, and the
log spam pushed a frame late -- max delta 42.15 ms against 33.68 ms before,
3x+=1 where every prior run had zero.

What that measured is worth keeping: this latch is consumed at VSYNC, not
immediately. It reads 1 for up to a refresh period by design, so any bound must
exceed one frame period. The original 50 ms is roughly three, which is margin
rather than sloppiness.

Also documented: the four slots are a hardware ring DECD advances through with
a different frame in each, but this driver writes the same address to all four.
That is correct for a page-flip model and is measured working; it also means
the ring provides no buffering, so anyone adding queueing to absorb jitter has
to change it first. That is a landmine, not a bug.

And a hazard note on wait_ready(): readl_poll_timeout() sleeps. Fine from the
commit worker, which is the only caller, but moving plane updates into the
vblank handler -- the obvious next optimisation -- would make it a
sleep-in-atomic bug.

OUT OF SERIES. Apply after 0080.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -181,6 +181,22 @@
 	u32 ready;
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * HAZARD: readl_poll_timeout() SLEEPS. That is fine from the atomic
+	 * commit worker, which is the only caller today, but moving plane
+	 * updates into the vblank handler -- the obvious next optimisation --
+	 * would make this a sleep-in-atomic bug. Switch to the _atomic variant
+	 * first if that ever happens.
+	 *
+	 * 50 ms is deliberate and must stay above one frame period. MEASURED:
+	 * this latch is consumed at VSYNC, not immediately -- tightening the
+	 * bound to 2 ms on the theory that it clears in ~75 us (which is DECD's
+	 * ring-arm figure, carried across by analogy and wrong for this
+	 * register) produced 275 "READY did not clear" warnings in a single
+	 * 10-second run, and the resulting log spam pushed a frame late. The
+	 * latch reads 1 for up to a refresh period by design; 50 ms is roughly
+	 * three, which is margin rather than sloppiness.
+	 */
 	ret = readl_poll_timeout(h->regs + reg, ready, !ready, 100, 50000);
 	if (ret)
 		drm_warn(&h->drm, "%s READY did not clear (%08x)\n",
@@ -298,6 +314,20 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * MEASURED, so do not "optimise" the two things that look expensive here.
+ *
+ * This function rewrites all per-stream state on every commit -- geometry,
+ * both strides, the ring-mux word, all four queue slots and the dirty latch --
+ * when a page-flip only needs the Y/C addresses to change. It also calls
+ * h713_afbd_wait_ready() twice on the enable path. Both look like they should
+ * cost frames. They do not: the plane sustains 59.71 fps on a 59.97 Hz panel
+ * with every flip landing on its own vsync (n=298, sd 0.03 ms, zero drops),
+ * and PTS-paced 29.97 fps content runs with nothing late.
+ *
+ * Splitting first-enable from per-flip updates is therefore a change with no
+ * problem to solve. Measure before touching it.
+ */
 static void h713_afbd_video_atomic_update(struct drm_plane *plane,
 					  struct drm_atomic_state *state)
 {
@@ -329,6 +359,13 @@
 	/*
 	 * The live display service consumes a complete four-entry queue. A
 	 * repeated frame is the proven static representation of one KMS buffer.
+	 *
+	 * Note what this is NOT: the four slots are a hardware ring that DECD
+	 * advances through with a different frame in each. Here every slot gets
+	 * the same address, so the ring is used as one buffer replicated four
+	 * times. That is correct for a page-flip model and is measured working,
+	 * but anyone adding queueing to absorb jitter must change this first --
+	 * the ring currently provides no buffering at all.
 	 */
 	for (i = 0; i < H713_VIDEO_SLOTS; i++) {
 		writel(lower_32_bits(y), h->regs + AFBD_VIDEO_Y0 + i * 4);
